 >>[2026-09-28 17:08] verify — spw resolve now tries file-relative then consumer-root, matching the LSP, and reports basis; plan refs missing fell from 1183 to 32 of 1396. Parity evidence for gate 0; landed via relationship-contours.
 >>[2026-09-30 16:54] revise — hold Gate 0's journey choice. Resolve parity stands. A newcomer may arrive on one fragment; do not design the atlas as a command tour, and do not lock host matrices to current verb names.
 >>[2026-10-03 17:20] verify — Anchor-line navigation, wire: textDocument/definition and textDocument/documentLink through navigation.ts, fed by the ServerIndex anchor entries from scanWorkspace, save, and watched-file refresh; references only routes through splitAnchor. Cost, medians on four files: documentLinks is at or below main once targets are indexed (curriculum-dag 28.2 to 16.9 ms; a reviewer reproduced the 0.6 ratio at load 51); round 2 had cost seconds per request. Review approved with nits.
 >>[2026-10-03 17:35] implement — Took the review fixes: a link listing reads only the index and never parses, so the cold window before the first scan links the file instead of parsing every target (manifest 152 ms cold at load 32 to 51, against about 29 s before); go to definition still parses an unindexed target once per file version, from disk, so a discarded buffer never answers; the open-buffer lookup no longer refreshes a closed buffer; the parse memo is least-recently-used. LSP suite 283 of 284 with the known form-context failure; both fixes are mutation-checked.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:25] revise — Reconciled commits against history (14b4b477, 2c082311, 861fa214 landed) and routed the vscode-lsp-roadmap consumer asks: parser-first selectPathRefs, lens peek, routes and leading slash, willRenameFiles, probe fixtures; alias binder kept as a question.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:25] observe — selectPathRefs still runs fallbackPathRefs first (spw-selector.ts:301); lens command is still empty in display.ts; no willRenameFiles handler; documentSymbols already returns nested children via outlineFromSource, so the roadmap row calling the outline flat looks stale.
 >>[2026-10-12 15:37] revise — Commits named per the convention in 90c58bbc: 3 landed, 6 planned, 0 dropped.
 >>[2026-10-12 20:09] implement — Landed: parser-first citations (e6c56978) dropped 161 false citations over 1306 files; path moves rewrite citations through willRenameFiles (56844e6e). VS Code lenses open a references peek client-side (99e6d993); the server-side lens command in lens_reference_peek is still planned.
 >>[2026-10-13 11:35] decide — The owner chose the p8 ref form, a root with a fragment (@tree#src_x), from syntax-hardware-alignment, and routed it with refs outside strings to this plan: parser_first_refs, resolve parity, anchor-line fragments, and rename rewrites already live here. p8 is not grammar invention: the form parses today, so the work is teaching the readers to read it.
 >>[2026-10-13 11:35] observe — packages/spw-lsp/src/spw-selector.ts reads a parser Reference node only when it holds a slash, so @tree#src_x is dropped; its step-3 rootRef regex reads a root followed by a path anywhere, strings included, and the LSP raised an unresolved-reference warning on such a form quoted inside a plan string on 2026-10-13. The roots it needs, @caches, @tree, and @journal, are declared in .spw/workspace.spw, so a named root is a stated binding, not the file-scoped alias ?[alias_binder] asks about.
 >>[2026-10-13 11:35] note — Fixture for ~[refs_outside_strings]: a plan string that quotes a root followed by a path must raise no diagnostic, no link, and no graph edge.
 >>[2026-10-13 11:37] observe — Owner screenshot of this wip.spw in VS Code, 2026-10-13. One: stream lines 59 to 70 render the >> marker and body plain while 71 to 79 carry colored markers, figures, and commit refs, though their bytes match; the apostrophe rule in spw.tmLanguage.json does not explain it, and the split looks like semantic tokens reaching only part of the document. Two: inside entry prose, textDocument/definition, a method name, and a bare repo path both take path color; a bare word with a slash is prose, not a figure. Three: code lenses read 7 topic and 2 topic, without the plural and without saying what a topic counts.
 >>[2026-10-13 11:37] decide — ~[refs_outside_strings] covers the editors too: a word with a slash in prose or in a string takes no path color in TextMate or semantic tokens. The split rendering of stream lines goes to plan-stream-parse-health, which owns entry reading; the lens label is folded into ~[lens_reference_peek], which already rewrites those lenses in display.ts.
 >>[2026-10-13 11:59] revise — lens_reference_peek is dropped: VS Code's peek landed client-side in 99e6d993, the server command route belongs to vscode-authoring-probe-loop code_lens_commands, and lens labels move to editor-reading-hierarchy frame_lens_units. Path color in prose moves to editor-reading-hierarchy path_color_parsed_only; refs_outside_strings keeps the LSP readers, links, diagnostics, and graph edges.
 >>[2026-10-13 12:11] revise — Two citation defects from the caches join this plan: resolve accepts frame names and binding keys as fragments (resolve-citation.ts 76 to 87), so resolve_anchor_strict lands with root_anchor_refs; expand projects 1 of 21 targets of a reflexive edge, so expand_reflexive_targets. The cost sample for root_anchor_refs has a baseline: resolve over the caches tree took 186 s on 2026-10-13.
 >>[2026-10-13 20:52] note — Evidence for ?[alias_binder]: the owner's scratch notes read better once a file-scoped roots frame named each plan path once (@hashes, @pointers) and the notes cited the names; plan wip.spw roots frames already work this way.
 >>[2026-10-25 20:40] note — Evidence for ?[alias_binder] from a mounted consumer (2026-10-25, rank 4, alias_and_hover): a file-scoped alias cannot be followed, and the consumer asks whether an alias jumps to its declaration first or straight to the file it names. Its rank 11 ask, a declared handle linking inside a string, is the same binder read in prose; it lands with refs_outside_strings rules intact (no path link, no diagnostic, no graph edge).
 >>[2026-10-25 21:33] implement — Uncommitted on main 2026-10-25, for consumer rank 11: new prose-handles.ts links a handle this file declares (a parser-vouched citation right after @name:) inside a STRING or PHRASE token, through the same citationTarget its declaration link uses; definition follows it too, and the hover leads with the declaration and keeps geometry last. refs_outside_strings rules hold: no selector hit, diagnostic, graph edge or path color, and a root with a path or an anchor inside a string stays text. On the consumer: 1131 uses in 183 files link.
 >>[2026-10-25 21:33] observe — Two resolver changes rode with the citation lenses: a citation with a leading slash resolves from the workspace root, not the disk root (consumer @page and @registry forms; its unresolved citations fall from 1688 to 267 with the scope change), and the reference graph walks only files inside the index scope, so a mounted _workbench, release copies and .tmp leave spw/referenceGraph (consumer surfaces 2534 to 1391; the first build had taken 78 s, nearly all parsing the mounted workbench).
 >>[2026-10-25 21:33] observe — Tension for resolve_anchor_strict: the consumer route trees cite binding keys as fragments (follows to _family.spw#shape_1, shape_1 = .{ ... }), 8 per family. resolve_anchor_strict would make those fragments fail; the new key lens counts only #> anchors, so those citations count toward the file total alone. Either the consumer writes #>shape_1 anchors or fragment lookup widens to binding keys; the consumer asked which in lens_counts_citations.
}
